---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/loyalty';
const title = 'Loyalty SMS: points, rewards and member messaging | Shopify SMS Marketing Apps';
const desc = 'Plan loyalty messages around verified member identity, available rewards, permission, redemption exits and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Loyalty messaging</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Send loyalty messages that match the member’s real rewards</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A purchase, a points award and an available reward are different events. Build SMS around the reward a member can actually use, with a clear account destination and a reason to stop sending once the situation changes.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore messaging apps</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate email and SMS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Start with member identity and a ledger owner</h2>
      <p>Choose one program system to authorize points, tiers, reward issuance, redemption and expiry. Messaging tools receive projections of that ledger; a marketing tag should not create a balance or promote a member into a tier. Keep the member ID, store customer ID, order ID, ledger transaction ID and current program-rule version together so an operator can explain why a message was eligible.</p>
      <p>The payer, account holder and gift recipient may be different people. Attribute an order under the program’s published earning rules; do not award the delivery recipient points merely because their phone appears on shipping details. Reconcile guest checkouts and account merges before showing a balance. Ambiguous matches go to account recovery or support, rather than disclosing one person’s rewards to another.</p>
      <p>Shopify documents that customer profiles can originate from orders, account or mailing-list signups, and abandoned checkouts. A profile’s existence therefore does not establish loyalty membership. See <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/customers/manage-customers">Shopify’s customer management documentation</a>. Keep membership, channel permission and reward eligibility as separate fields.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Store earned, pending, available, redeemed, expired and reversed entries separately; retain adjustment reasons.</li>
        <li>Give tier status an effective date, qualification period and rules version. A historical tier-change event is not proof of today’s access.</li>
        <li>Record event time, ingestion time and ledger version; resolve out-of-order changes against authoritative current state.</li>
        <li>Keep account links authenticated and avoid exposing full balances or redeemable credentials in shared-device previews.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Match the message to the reward state</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Useful message job</th><th>Exit or block</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Order awaiting settlement or program approval</td><td>Explain pending points in the account if needed</td><td>Do not call them spendable or promise a tier upgrade</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Available points reach a reward threshold</td><td>Invite an eligible member to view redemption options</td><td>Block if available balance falls below threshold</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reward issued but unused</td><td>Explain its scope, restrictions and account destination</td><td>Stop on redemption, cancellation, expiry or replacement</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tier becomes effective</td><td>Explain the benefits now active under that version</td><td>Recheck status before claiming access or free benefits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Return, partial refund or points reversal</td><td>Provide an accurate account explanation through the appropriate service route</td><td>Pause conflicting promotional reward jobs until reconciled</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Earned points are not a redeemed reward. If redemption converts points to a voucher, use the voucher’s own ID and status for reminders. Check minimum spend, eligible products, combination rules and remaining uses before describing it as usable. A refund may reverse earnings without restoring a spent voucher; apply the program’s actual policy instead of assuming either behavior.</p>
      <p>For expiry messaging, identify exactly what expires: a points lot, a voucher or a tier benefit. Show the correct date and timezone from the governing rules. If an extension changes the expiry, cancel the old reminder and evaluate a new one. Do not invent a deadline when the ledger has none, or imply all points expire because one lot does.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Membership does not grant messaging permission</h2>
      <p>Gate promotional loyalty SMS on current permission for that destination and message purpose. Preserve capture source, disclosure, timestamp and subsequent changes. Membership, a phone number on an order and an email subscription are separate facts. Apply recipient-specific sending restrictions and opt-out handling for the markets and providers you use; a fixed store-wide schedule is insufficient evidence that a send is appropriate.</p>
      <p>Separate requested account assistance from a reward promotion. Calling an offer a “balance update” does not decide its service status. Have the responsible team review the purpose, content and applicable requirements. A member who cannot receive promotional SMS should still be able to access their rewards through an authenticated account and request help. Use email only where that channel is also appropriate and permitted.</p>
      <p>Coordinate with <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">permission controls</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">recipient sending windows</a>. These operational controls do not themselves establish legal compliance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Own the queue across journeys</h2>
      <p>Evaluate member identity, permission, ledger freshness, current reward version, expiry, destination and suppression immediately before dispatch. Use a dedupe key scoped to member, reward or points lot, message purpose and version. Preserve dispatch history when retrying so duplicate events cannot produce duplicate reminders. A new ledger version should trigger reevaluation, not automatically authorize another text.</p>
      <p>Redemption cancels unused-reward reminders. A purchase cancels purchase-prompt jobs tied to the completed opportunity; a genuine new earning event can be evaluated separately after the ledger updates. An open dispute, missing reward or refund support case pauses promotional nudges that would contradict assistance. Opt-outs cancel affected promotional queues even if a reward remains active.</p>
      <p>Give loyalty, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/winback">winback</a> and launch journeys a shared contact budget and priority rule. If a cart discount and earned reward compete, choose an eligible offer under combination and margin rules rather than stacking both by accident. Assign one channel the reminder; use the other only for a distinct useful job. Set contact limits from the audience promise and observed response, without a universal monthly quota.</p>
      <p>During a ledger outage, hold balance, tier and expiry claims. Alert the program owner, reconcile current state, discard expired or already-satisfied jobs and release only still-useful messages under a bounded recovery window. If delivery status is unknown, reconcile the provider receipt before retrying. Recovery should not replay an entire stale backlog.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios and proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are proposed checks for a store’s implementation, not merchant results or claims that a vendor already passes them.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Gift payer and recipient:</strong> an order uses the member payer’s account and a recipient’s shipping phone. Expect credit only under the payer’s earning rules and no loyalty SMS to the recipient based on shipping details.</li>
        <li><strong>Pending purchase:</strong> a purchase would add 200 points but settlement is pending. Expect the available balance to exclude those points and no “reward unlocked” text until the ledger authorizes them.</li>
        <li><strong>Refund before dispatch:</strong> a partial refund reverses enough points to remove eligibility after a job is queued. Expect the final eligibility check to cancel it and retain the reason.</li>
        <li><strong>Redeemed reward:</strong> a voucher is used while its reminder waits. Expect cancellation even if a delayed “voucher issued” event subsequently arrives.</li>
        <li><strong>Expiry extension:</strong> a points lot gains a new deadline under an approved adjustment. Expect the old version’s expiry reminder to be blocked and any new reminder to describe only that lot’s current deadline.</li>
        <li><strong>Permission and support:</strong> a member opts out of promotional SMS while reporting missing points. Expect promotional jobs to stop, with account access and requested support handled through an appropriate route.</li>
        <li><strong>Duplicate and stale tier events:</strong> replay a tier upgrade twice, then deliver an older event after a downgrade. Expect no duplicate announcement and no claim to benefits absent from current status.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery and conflict:</strong> restore the ledger after an outage while a cart journey has already produced a purchase. Expect reconciliation and cancellation of stale purchase prompts, rather than simultaneous cart and loyalty reminders.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Choose a messaging setup around the program</h2>
      <p>A unified email and SMS workspace can fit when one team owns ledger mapping, permission and suppression across both channels. Separate senders can fit when each has a clear job and a shared decision service prevents conflicts. An account-and-email-led setup can fit when benefits need explanation and there is no useful time-sensitive SMS action. None of these arrangements makes a marketing platform the points authority.</p>
      <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> to make a shortlist. Ask each candidate to demonstrate your fixtures with the actual loyalty integration: stable member IDs, current balance reads, cancellation, rule-version updates, opt-out propagation and retry receipts. Confirm current integration scope, plan availability and costs directly with the supplier. A generic custom-event trigger is not proof that reward reversals or redemption exits work.</p>
      <p>Before migration, reconcile balances and reward IDs, preserve permission and suppression evidence, and transfer dispatch history. Freeze overlapping senders during cutover. Resume only after sample members agree between the program ledger and messaging projections.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Measure rewards after their cost</h2>
      <p>Randomize eligible members into message and holdout groups before sending, with stable assignment across channels. Both groups retain the same earned benefits and access; the test changes the reminder. Track redemptions, repeat purchases, returns, support load and opt-outs over a window long enough for the program’s purchase and refund cycle. Report uncertainty and contamination from other journeys, rather than treating attributed sales as incremental lift.</p>
      <p>Calculate contribution using net sales after discounts and refunds, less product, fulfillment and variable service costs. Include reward fulfillment or subsidy costs that are not already reflected in those lines. Separate points issuance and outstanding obligations from realized redemption costs; align valuation with the finance owner. Do not subtract a discount twice when net sales already includes it.</p>
      <p><strong>Hypothetical arithmetic:</strong> 1,000 eligible messaged members generate $1,400 contribution before messaging costs; 1,000 holdout members generate $1,100 on the same basis. The observed difference is $0.30 per eligible member, or $300 at the treated group’s size. Subtract $180 of incremental sending and allocated operating costs to obtain $120. This example assumes reward costs are already included in contribution; it is not evidence of a reliable effect. Check uncertainty, later returns and outstanding reward obligations before scaling.</p>
      <p>Compare pricing at your expected audience and peak send volume, including integration maintenance, message segments, provider fees and reward support. See the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline guide</a>. A high redemption rate alone can mean members used benefits they would have redeemed without a reminder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Before activating a loyalty reminder</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Confirm the member match and authoritative ledger version.</li>
        <li>Verify spendable balance or active reward, restrictions and the exact expiry being described.</li>
        <li>Check destination permission, sending window, holdout and cross-journey priority.</li>
        <li>Confirm redemption, purchase, support and opt-out exits invalidate queued jobs.</li>
        <li>Assign owners for reconciliation, unknown delivery, missing rewards and rollback.</li>
        <li>Approve the contribution worksheet and observation window before interpreting results.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Use <a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">VIP segmentation</a> for audience planning and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase messaging</a> for the order relationship. Keep both subordinate to the member’s current program state.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
